date -u did putenv("TZ=UTC0") with a string LITERAL.  putenv keeps the
pointer, and AMIX libc.so.1's TZ parser (static getzname, reached from
tzset/localtime) temporarily stores a NUL into the TZ string in place.
GCC 14 puts literals in read-only .rodata, so every date -u died with
SIGSEGV writing there (measured on the box: PC in getzname, clrb %a2@,
fault address = the "0" of the literal).  Use a writable static array.

--- a/src/date.c	2004-02-05 11:36:07.000000000 +0200
+++ b/src/date.c	2026-10-29 23:01:26.035008617 +0200
@@ -343,8 +343,13 @@
 	/* POSIX says that `date -u' is equivalent to setting the TZ
 	   environment variable, so this option should do nothing other
 	   than setting TZ.  */
-	if (putenv ("TZ=UTC0") != 0)
-	  xalloc_die ();
+	{
+	  /* Writable: AMIX libc's TZ parser (getzname) stores a NUL into
+	     the TZ string in place, and putenv keeps the pointer.  */
+	  static char tz_utc0[] = "TZ=UTC0";
+	  if (putenv (tz_utc0) != 0)
+	    xalloc_die ();
+	}
 	TZSET;
 	break;
       case_GETOPT_HELP_CHAR;
